  <h1>Heterogeneous-Agent Neoclassical Macroeconomic Models</h1>

  <p>
    This workshop page outlines a research program focused on 
    <strong>heterogeneous-agent real business cycle (HA-RBC)</strong> 
    models within a fully micro-founded, neoclassical DSGE framework.
    The emphasis is on differences in <em>time preferences</em> (discount factors)
    and <em>risk preferences</em>, and how these shape savings behavior,
    occupational choice, capital accumulation, and aggregate dynamics.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>1. Core Economic Environment</h2>

  <p>
    Households solve a standard intertemporal optimization problem:
  </p>

  <pre><code>
Max  E₀ ∑ βᵢ^t u(cᵢ,t)

s.t.

aᵢ,t+1 = (1 + r_t)aᵢ,t + w_t lᵢ,t − cᵢ,t
aᵢ,t+1 ≥ borrowing constraint
  </code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li>βᵢ : heterogeneous discount factors (time preference)</li>
    <li>u(c) : heterogeneous risk aversion (e.g. CRRA with γᵢ)</li>
    <li>Income risk and productivity shocks follow Markov processes</li>
    <li>Competitive firms: Y_t = A_t F(K_t, L_t)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Aggregate dynamics emerge from the distribution of assets and preferences.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>2. Heterogeneity Structure</h2>

  <p>Agents differ in:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discount factor (βᵢ)</strong> → patience, savings behavior</li>
    <li><strong>Risk aversion (γᵢ)</strong> → portfolio choice, precautionary saving</li>
    <li><strong>Income risk exposure</strong> → stochastic productivity states</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Wealth inequality and occupational sorting arise endogenously
    from differences in patience and risk tolerance.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>3. Example: CRRA Utility</h2>

  <pre><code>
u(c) = (c^(1 − γᵢ) − 1) / (1 − γᵢ)
  </code></pre>

  <p>
    Higher γᵢ → more risk averse → stronger precautionary savings motive.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>4. Numerical Solution (Julia Placeholder)</h2>

  <p>
    Value function iteration for a heterogeneous agent:
  </p>

```julia
function bellman(V, β, γ, r, w, grid_a, shock_transition)
    V_new = similar(V)
    for (i, a) in enumerate(grid_a)
        value_choices = []
        for a_next in grid_a
            c = (1 + r) * a + w - a_next
            if c > 0
                u = (c^(1 - γ) - 1) / (1 - γ)
                continuation = β * V[i]  # placeholder expectation
                push!(value_choices, u + continuation)
            end
        end
        V_new[i] = maximum(value_choices)
    end
    return V_new
end
